---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Brief = `Everyone at the open evening needs a printed badge. Same
layout every time, just a different name and role on
each one. I have about forty people.`;

const SampleOutput = `+---------------------------+
|  AVERY OKONKWO            |
|  Robotics Club            |
+---------------------------+
+---------------------------+
|  BLAKE RIVERA             |
|  Debate Team              |
+---------------------------+`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-5" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back to Set 5
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge: Name Badge</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="brief">
          <h2>The Brief</h2>

          <Code code={Brief} frame="code" lang="txt" title="What they asked for" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>Write your requirements list before you write any code.</b> Input,
            calculate, output, and anything you had to decide for yourself. See{" "}
            <a href="/courses/ap-csp/unit-2/cc-set-5">Set 5</a> for what that looks like.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="notes">
          <h2>Notes</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>One procedure, called forty times &mdash; or three, to prove it works.</li>
            <li>The badge layout lives in <b>one</b> place. If you copy-paste the layout, you have missed the point.</li>
            <li>Two things change per badge. Those are your parameters.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="where-to-start">
          <h2>Where to Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Only one-star challenges have this section.</b> If you already know how to
            begin, skip it &mdash; and when you move up to two stars, working out the order
            yourself is part of the job.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>Write the badge for one hard-coded person first. Get the layout right.</li>
            <li>Now wrap it in <code>function badge() { }</code> and call it once. Same output.</li>
            <li>Find the two things that should differ per person and turn them into parameters.</li>
            <li>Call it three times with different arguments.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>Sample Output</h2>

          <Code code={SampleOutput} frame="terminal" title="Sample Output" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-3" />

          <p class="mt-3">Your layout can differ. What must be true is that changing a badge means changing an argument, never the procedure.</p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>
            Your requirements list and your program file, named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">cc-name-badge</b>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1 class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash">
          Commence Challenge
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
